					read-more-wrapper-module-scss-module__fIkcbW__hasOverFlow" data-testid="read-more-content"><div><div>Inscriptions identify the subject of this amulet as the Woman with the Issue of Blood (Mark 5:25–34; Luke 8:43–48). On the reverse a woman stands in the orant (prayer) pose. Hematite was believed to stop the flow of blood and was a favorite medium for amulets related to female reproductive health and menstrual problems.</div></div></div></div></div></section></main><aside class="object-page-top-module-scss-module__B1ut-G__aside"><div class="image-viewer-module-scss-module__FEM1gW__wrapper"><section class="image-viewer-module-scss-module__FEM1gW__imageAndControls"><div class="image-viewer-module-scss-module__FEM1gW__imageAndRightsContainer"><figure itemType="http://schema.org/ImageObject" class="image-viewer-module-scss-module__FEM1gW__figure"><img fetchPriority="high" loading="eager" role="button" alt="Amulet Carved in Intaglio (Incised), Hematite, silver mount, Byzantine (Egypt)" class="image-viewer-module-scss-module__FEM1gW__featuredImage image-viewer-module-scss-module__FEM1gW__isButton" src="https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/api/collection/v1/iiif/464456/2169724/main-image" itemProp="contentUrl"/></figure><div class="image-viewer-module-scss-module__FEM1gW__featuredImageMeta"><div class="image-viewer-module-scss-module__FEM1gW__featuredImageAccess"></div></div><div class="image-buttons-module-scss-module__0Iy8yW__container"><span class="image-buttons-module-scss-module__0Iy8yW__oaWrapper"><div><span class="tooltip-module-scss-module___bPAea__button"><button class="icon-button-module-scss-module__WSsQUW__button icon-button-module-scss-module__WSsQUW__small icon-button-module-scss-module__WSsQUW__lightMode open-access-info-module-scss-module__0aAotq__button" type="button"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="1em" height="1em" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 25"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9.284 15.115c-1.534 0-2.6-1.166-2.6-2.75 0-1.6 1.066-2.75 2.6