 Tuerk. \"An Early Byzantine Inscribed Amulet and Its Narratives.\" \u003ci\u003eByzantine and Modern Greek Studies\u003c/i\u003e (1999). pp. 25–42, fig. 1, 2.\u003cbr/\u003e\u003cbr/\u003eKalavrezou, Ioli. \u003ci\u003eByzantine Women and their World\u003c/i\u003e. Cambridge, MA: Harvard Art Museums, 2003. pp. 274, 283–284.\u003cbr/\u003e\u003cbr/\u003e\u003ci\u003eLes croix-reliquaires pectorales byzantines en bronze\u003c/i\u003e. Bibliothèque des cahiers archéologiques, Vol. 16. Paris: Picard, 2006. p. 71 n. 270.\u003cbr/\u003e\u003cbr/\u003eBaert, Barbara, Liesbet Kusters,  and Emma Sidgwick. \"An Issue of Blood: The Healing of the Woman with the Haemorrhage (Mark 5.24B-34; Luke 8.42B-48; Matthew 9.19-22) in Early Medieval Visual Culture.\" \u003ci\u003eJournal of Religion and Health\u003c/i\u003e 51, no. 3 (2012). fig. 11, pp. 674–76.\u003cbr/\u003e\u003cbr/\u003eBaert, Barbara. \"The Woman with the Blood Flow (Mark 5:24-34): Narrative, Iconic, and Anthropological Spaces.\" In \u003ci\u003eGeneral Introduction: Touching the Hem\u003c/i\u003e, edited by Barbara Baert. Leuven/Louvain, BE: Peeters, 2014. pl. 8, pp. 17-20.\u003cbr/\u003e\u003cbr/\u003eKnipp, David. \"The Woman with the Blood Flow (Mark 5:24-34): Narrative, Iconic, and Anthropological Spaces.\" In \u003ci\u003eNarrative and Symbol: The Early Christian Image of the Haemorrhoissa and the Mosaics in the Narthex of the Kariye Camii\u003c/i\u003e, edited by Barbara Baert. Leuven, BE: Peeters, 2014. pl. 2, pp. 146-147.\u003cbr/\u003e\u003cbr/\u003eJensen, Robin M.,  and Mark D. Ellison. \u003ci\u003eThe Routledge Handbook of Early Christian Art\u003c/i\u003e. New York: Routledge, 2018. fig.17.8, p. 285.\u003cbr/\u003e\u003cbr/\u003eSchwartz, Ellen, ed. \u003ci\u003eOxford Handbook on Byzantine Art and Architecture\u003c/i\u003e. New York, NY: Oxford University Press, 2021. pp. 109–110.\u003cbr/\u003e\u003cbr/\u003eStonberg, Jacquelyn Tuerk. \"Magic and Byzantine Art.\" In \u003ci\u003eOxford Handbook on Byzantine Art and Architecture\u003c/i\u003e, edited by Ellen Schwartz. New York, NY: Oxford University Press, 2021. pp. 87–90, figs. 6.1, 6.2, pl. 3A.\u003cbr/\u003e\u003cbr/\u003eStonberg, Jacquelyn Tuerk. \"Magical Amulets, Magical Thinking, and Semiotics in Early Byzantium.\" \u003ci\u003eOld World: Journal of Ancient Africa and Eurasia\u003c/i\u003e 1 (2021). pp. 6–9, fig. 5–6.\u003cbr/\u003e\u003cbr/\u003eAchi, Andrea. \u003ci\u003eAfrica and Byzantium\u003c/i\u003e. New York: The Metropolitan Museum of Art, 2023. fig. 11, pp. 49–51.\u003cbr/\u003e\u003cbr/\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"35:[\"$\",\"$L2d\",\"464456-References\",{\"maxLines\":25,\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"$46\"}}]}]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"39:[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":2,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"2022.164\",\"artistPrimary\":\"Alwar Balasubramaniam\",\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[{\"constituentID\":195182,\"displayName\":\"Alwar Balasubramaniam\",\"nationality\":\"Indian\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"role\":\"Artist\",\"roleID\":19}],\"artistsFlattened\":[\"Alwar Balasubramaniam\"],\"classifications\":[\"Sculpture\"],\"culture\":null,\"crdCollectionIds\":[21,887],\"crdId\":856733,\"creditLine\":\"Purchase, Dipti Mathur, Asha Jadeja, and Sunil and Blanca Hirani Gifts, 2022\",\"dated\":\"2011–15\",\"departmentName\":[\"Modern and Contemporary Art\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"modern-and-contemporary-art\"],\"dynasty\":null,\"description\":\"Appearing as a pile of human skin cast to the floor, this sculpture made of sandstone belies its material. To produce it, the artist recast a plaster form of his own body using fiberglass and molded rubber. Subsequent castings and manipulations resulted in this machine-carved and hand-finished version. Balasubramaniam began making casts of his body in the early 2000s. He describes these as \\\"traces\\\" and \\\"signs of a former presence … the sculpture is nothing but a trace of myself.\\\" Throughout his practice, the artist investigates the threshold between presence and absence, materiality and immateriality, the physical and spiritual, the object and space. In this unnerving sculpture, the body appears to be withered away, no longer a mass but a mere shell, suggesting the precariousness between life and death.\",\"extendedStatuses\":[\"accessioned\"],\"gallery\":\"302\",\"geography\":[],\"geolocation\":[\"Asia\",\"India\"],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":true,\"isOpenAccess\":false,\"materials\":[\"Sandstone\",\"Sculpture\"],\"medium\":\"Sandstone\",\"objectName\":\"Sculpture\",\"period\":null,\"provenance\":\"the artist, Tamilnadu, India (until 2022; sold through Talwar Gallery, New York to MMA)\",\"reign\":null,\"sortArtist\":\"balasubramaniam alwar\",\"sortTitle\":\"body as shell\",\"suggestionArtists\":[{\"value\":\"Alwar Balasubramaniam\"}],\"suggestionGeolocations\":[{\"value\":\"Asia\"},{\"value\":\"India\"}],\"suggestionMaterials\":[{\"value\":\"Sandstone\"},{\"value\":\"Sculpture\"}],\"tags\":[\"Human Figures\"],\"thumbnailAltText\":\"Body as Shell, Alwar Balasubramaniam  Indian, Sandstone\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/ma/web-additional/DP-24424-003.jpg\",\"tmsDepartmentId\":388,\"title\":[\"Body as Shell\"],\"whenFrom\":2011,\"whenTo\":2015,\"yearsMinMax\":[2011,2015],\"yearRange\":{\"gte\":2011,\"lte\":2015}}}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3a:[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":3,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"AFT.006\",\"artistPrimary\":null,\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[],\"artistsFlattened\":[],\"classifications\":[\"(not assigned)\"],\"culture\":null,\"crdCollectionIds\":[887,17],\"crdId\":894276,\"creditLine\":\"MoMA, Fund for the Twenty-First Century, 1108.2014\",\"dated\":\"2011-2012\",\"departmentName\":[\"Medieval Art and The Cloisters\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"medieval-art-and-the-cloisters\"],\"dynasty\":null,\"description\":null,\"extendedStatuses\":[\"exhibition_loan\"],\"gallery\":\"302\",\"geography\":[],\"geolocation\":[],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":true,\"isOpenAccess\":false,\"materials\":[],\"medium\":\"Audio recording of performance (28 min)\",\"objectName\":null,\"period\":null,\"provenance\":null,\"reign\":null,\"sortArtist\":null,\"sortTitle\":\"i want my spot back\",\"suggestionArtists\":[],\"suggestionGeolocations\":[],\"suggestionMaterials\":[],\"tags\":[],\"thumbnailAltText\":\"I Want My Spot Back, Audio recording of performance (28 min)\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/md/web-additional/AFT.006a.jpg\",\"tmsDepartmentId\":729,\"title\":[\"I Want My Spot Back\"],\"whenFrom\":2011,\"whenTo\":2012,\"yearsMinMax\":[2011,2012],\"yearRange\":{\"gte\":2011,\"lte\":2012}}}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3b:[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":4,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"AFT.004\",\"artistPrimary\":\"Adrian Piper\",\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[{\"constituentID\":162254,\"displayName\":\"Adrian Piper\",\"nationality\":\"American\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"role\":\"Artist\",\"roleID\":19}],\"artistsFlattened\":[\"Adrian Piper\"],\"classifications\":[\"Mirrors\"],\"culture\":null,\"crdCollectionIds\":[887,17],\"crdId\":892799,\"creditLine\":\"Purchase, Lila Acheson Wallace Gift, 2023\\r\\nPiper.2023.1\",\"dated\":\"2004\",\"departmentName\":[\"Medieval Art and The Cloisters\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"medieval-art-and-the-cloisters\"],\"dynasty\":null,\"description\":null,\"extendedStatuses\":[\"exhibition_loan\"],\"gallery\":\"302\",\"geography\":[],\"geolocation\":[\"North and Central America\",\"United States\"],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":true,\"isOpenAccess\":false,\"materials\":[\"Mirrors\",\"Metal\",\"Gold\",\"Engraving\",\"Wood\",\"Mahogany\",\"Frames\"],\"medium\":\"Oval mirror with gold leaf engraved text in mahogany frame.\",\"objectName\":null,\"period\":null,\"provenance\":\"In the Collection of the Adrian Piper Research Archive (APRA) Foundation\\r\\nBerlin.\",\"reign\":null,\"sortArtist\":\"piper adrian\",\"sortTitle\":\"everything 4 edition 6 of 8\",\"suggestionArtists\":[{\"value\":\"Adrian Piper\"}],\"suggestionGeolocations\":[{\"value\":\"North and Central America\"},{\"value\":\"United States\"}],\"suggestionMaterials\":[{\"value\":\"Mirrors\"},{\"value\":\"Metal\"},{\"value\":\"Gold\"},{\"value\":\"Engraving\"},{\"value\":\"Wood\"},{\"value\":\"Mahogany\"},{\"value\":\"Frames\"}],\"tags\":[],\"thumbnailAltText\":\"Everything #4, Edition 6 of 8, Adrian Piper  American, Oval mirror with gold leaf engraved text in mahogany frame.\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/md/web-additional/AFT.004a.jpg\",\"tmsDepartmentId\":729,\"title\":[\"Everything #4, Edition 6 of 8\"],\"whenFrom\":2004,\"whenTo\":2004,\"yearsMinMax\":[2004,2004],\"yearRange\":{\"gte\":2004,\"lte\":2004}}}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"47:T4d1,Everything #4 exemplifies the spiritual dimension of Adrian Piper’s rigorous conceptual art practice. This oval-shaped mirror is one of eight (three in The Met’s collection) similarly etched in gold with the phrase \"EVERYTHING WILL BE TAKEN AWAY,\" which is as much a reminder of mortality as a harbinger of transcendence. Mirrors have long been used as memento mori, or symbols of death, in the history of art. Everything #4 invites viewers to contemplate its otherworldly proclamation and their own reflection, in context. Seriality is key to the work, as its identical editions enable multiple encounters that mirror mantra-like repetition. The Met’s installation of six editions as part of Afterlives: Contemporary Art in the Byzantine Crypt (January 29, 2024-January 25, 2026) realized, for the first time, the artist’s preferred mode of exhibiting the work, \"dispersed among widely separated spaces and rooms, so that the viewer encounters this reminder of mortality and transcendence repeatedly but unexpectedly.\" [1][1] The artist quoted in Andrea Myers Achi and Akili Tommasino, \"Visualizing the Afterlife,\" Perspectives: Religion and Spirituality [Online resource: metmuseum.org/perspectives/articles], May 17, 2024."])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3c:[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":5,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"2024.7\",\"artistPrimary\":\"Adrian Piper\",\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[{\"constituentID\":162254,\"displayName\":\"Adrian Piper\",\"nationality\":\"American\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"role\":\"Artist\",\"roleID\":19}],\"artistsFlattened\":[\"Adrian Piper\"],\"classifications\":[\"Sculpture\"],\"culture\":null,\"crdCollectionIds\":[21],\"crdId\":900397,\"creditLine\":\"Purchase, Lila Acheson Wallace Gift, 2023\",\"dated\":\"2004\",\"departmentName\":[\"Modern and Contemporary Art\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"modern-and-contemporary-art\"],\"dynasty\":null,\"description\":\"$47\",\"extendedStatuses\":[\"accessioned\"],\"gallery\":\"302\",\"geography\":[],\"geolocation\":[\"North and Central America\",\"United States\"],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":true,\"isOpenAccess\":false,\"materials\":[\"Mirrors\",\"Metal\",\"Gold\",\"Engraving\",\"Wood\",\"Mahogany\",\"Frames\",\"Sculpture\"],\"medium\":\"Oval mirror with gold leaf engraved text in mahogany frame\",\"objectName\":\"Sculpture\",\"period\":null,\"provenance\":\"Adrian Piper Research Archive Foundation, Berlin (2004–24; sold to MMA)\",\"reign\":null,\"sortArtist\":\"piper adrian\",\"sortTitle\":\"everything 4\",\"suggestionArtists\":[{\"value\":\"Adrian Piper\"}],\"suggestionGeolocations\":[{\"value\":\"North and Central America\"},{\"value\":\"United States\"}],\"suggestionMaterials\":[{\"value\":\"Mirrors\"},{\"value\":\"Metal\"},{\"value\":\"Gold\"},{\"value\":\"Engraving\"},{\"value\":\"Wood\"},{\"value\":\"Mahogany\"},{\"value\":\"Frames\"},{\"value\":\"Sculpture\"}],\"tags\":[],\"thumbnailAltText\":\"Everything #4, Adrian Piper  American, Oval mirror with gold leaf engraved text in mahogany frame\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/api/collection/v1/iiif/900397/2192950/thumbnail\",\"tmsDepartmentId\":388,\"title\":[\"Everything #4\"],\"whenFrom\":2004,\"whenTo\":2004,\"yearsMinMax\":[2004,2004],\"yearRange\":{\"gte\":2004,\"lte\":2004}}}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3d:[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":6,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"07.228.40\",\"artistPrimary\":null,\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[],\"artistsFlattened\":[],\"classifications\":[\"Sculpture\"],\"culture\":null,\"crdCollectionIds\":[14],\"crdId\":445015,\"creditLine\":\"Rogers Fund, 1907\",\"dated\":\"6th–7th century\",\"departmentName\":[\"Islamic Art\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"islamic-art\"],\"dynasty\":null,\"description\":\"The monastic community of Bawit, founded in the 300s by Apa (Father) Apollo at a small village, grew to consist of numerous complexes with residences, chapels, and service structures and two large churches profusely decorated with sculptures and paintings, often reused from other sites, all affiliated with the Coptic church. The more important buildings were decorated with courses of sculpted stone freely combining floral and geometric motifs inspired by classical art – rosettes, acanthus leaves, meander patterns – with more recent Christian themes. These sculptural elements, demonstrating the Byzantine love of complex patterning with lush textures, were originally painted in rich, vibrant colors like those surviving in textiles.\",\"extendedStatuses\":[\"accessioned\"],\"gallery\":\"302\",\"geography\":[\"Egypt\",\"Bawit\"],\"geolocation\":[\"Africa\",\"Egypt\",\"Mersa Matrûh\",\"Bawiti\"],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":true,\"isOpenAccess\":true,\"materials\":[\"Limestone\",\"Sculpture\",\"Reliefs\",\"Fragments\"],\"medium\":\"Limestone; carved in relief\",\"objectName\":\"Fragment\",\"period\":null,\"provenance\":null,\"reign\":null,\"sortArtist\":null,\"sortTitle\":\"fragment from a post or lintel with leaves branching from a running vine\",\"suggestionArtists\":[],\"suggestionGeolocations\":[{\"value\":\"Africa\"},{\"value\":\"Egypt\"},{\"value\":\"Mersa Matrûh\"},{\"value\":\"Bawiti\"}],\"suggestionMaterials\":[{\"value\":\"Limestone\"},{\"value\":\"Sculpture\"},{\"value\":\"Reliefs\"},{\"value\":\"Fragments\"}],\"tags\":[],\"thumbnailAltText\":\"Fragment from a Post or Lintel with Leaves Branching from a Running Vine\\r\\n, Limestone; carved in relief\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/is/web-additional/2828.jpg\",\"tmsDepartmentId\":318,\"title\":[\"Fragment from a Post or Lintel with Leaves Branching from a Running Vine\\r\\n\"],\"whenFrom\":500,\"whenTo\":699,\"yearsMinMax\":[500,699],\"yearRange\":{\"gte\":500,\"lte\":699}}}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3e:[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":7,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"08.246\",\"artistPrimary\":null,\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[],\"artistsFlattened\":[],\"classifications\":[\"Sculpture\"],\"culture\":null,\"crdCollectionIds\":[14],\"crdId\":445365,\"creditLine\":\"Rogers Fund, 1908\",\"dated\":\"6th–7th century\",\"departmentName\":[\"Islamic Art\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"islamic-art\"],\"dynasty\":null,\"description\":\"Funerary stelae from the Byzantine period in Egypt, carved in stone and usually painted, were permanent monuments to the deceased. While normally embedded in walls or floors near the tomb, some were part of larger structures.\",\"extendedStatuses\":[\"accessioned\"],\"gallery\":\"302\",\"geography\":[\"Egypt\",\"Hermonthis (now Armant)\"],\"geolocation\":[\"Africa\",\"Egypt\",\"Upper Egypt\",\"Armant\"],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":true,\"isOpenAccess\":true,\"materials\":[\"Sandstone\",\"Sculpture\",\"Stelae\"],\"medium\":\"Sandstone; carved, painted\",\"objectName\":\"Stele\",\"period\":null,\"provenance\":null,\"reign\":null,\"sortArtist\":null,\"sortTitle\":\"funerary stele with cross medallion\",\"suggestionArtists\":[],\"suggestionGeolocations\":[{\"value\":\"Africa\"},{\"value\":\"Egypt\"},{\"value\":\"Upper Egypt\"},{\"value\":\"Armant\"}],\"suggestionMaterials\":[{\"value\":\"Sandstone\"},{\"value\":\"Sculpture\"},{\"value\":\"Stelae\"}],\"tags\":[\"Cross\"],\"thumbnailAltText\":\"Funerary Stele with Cross Medallion, Sandstone; carved, painted\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/is/web-additional/08.246.JPG\",\"tmsDepartmentId\":318,\"title\":[\"Funerary Stele with Cross Medallion\"],\"whenFrom\":500,\"whenTo\":699,\"yearsMinMax\":[500,699],\"yearRange\":{\"gte\":500,\"lte\":699}}}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3f:[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":8,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"09.217.1a, b\",\"artistPrimary\":null,\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[],\"artistsFlattened\":[],\"classifications\":[\"Sculpture\"],\"culture\":null,\"crdCollectionIds\":[14],\"crdId\":445964,\"creditLine\":\"Rogers Fund, 1909\",\"dated\":\"6th century\",\"departmentName\":[\"Islamic Art\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"islamic-art\"],\"dynasty\":null,\"description\":\"This fragment of a frieze preserves some of the colorful glass-paste insets that once would have adorned many of the architectural stone fragments from Byzantine Egypt.\",\"extendedStatuses\":[\"accessioned\"],\"gallery\":\"302\",\"geography\":[\"Egypt\",\"Bawit\"],\"geolocation\":[\"Africa\",\"Egypt\",\"Mersa Matrûh\",\"Bawiti\"],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":true,\"isOpenAccess\":true,\"materials\":[\"Limestone\",\"Sculpture\",\"Reliefs\",\"Friezes\",\"Fragments\"],\"medium\":\"Limestone; carved in relief\",\"objectName\":\"Frieze fragment\",\"period\":null,\"provenance\":null,\"reign\":null,\"sortArtist\":null,\"sortTitle\":\"fragment from a frieze with meander pattern and diamondshaped rosettes\",\"suggestionArtists\":[],\"suggestionGeolocations\":[{\"value\":\"Africa\"},{\"value\":\"Egypt\"},{\"value\":\"Mersa Matrûh\"},{\"value\":\"Bawiti\"}],\"suggestionMaterials\":[{\"value\":\"Limestone\"},{\"value\":\"Sculpture\"},{\"value\":\"Reliefs\"},{\"value\":\"Friezes\"},{\"value\":\"Fragments\"}],\"tags\":[],\"thumbnailAltText\":\"Fragment from a Frieze with Meander Pattern and Diamond-Shaped Rosettes, Limestone; carved in relief\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/is/web-additional/09.217.1a%2C%20b.JPG\",\"tmsDepartmentId\":318,\"title\":[\"Fragment from a Frieze with Meander Pattern and Diamond-Shaped Rosettes\"],\"whenFrom\":500,\"whenTo\":599,\"yearsMinMax\":[500,599],\"yearRange\":{\"gte\":500,\"lte\":599}}}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"40:[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":9,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"09.217.3\",\"artistPrimary\":null,\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[],\"artistsFlattened\":[],\"classifications\":[\"Sculpture\"],\"culture\":null,\"crdCollectionIds\":[14],\"crdId\":445965,\"creditLine\":\"Rogers Fund, 1909\",\"dated\":\"5th–6th century\",\"departmentName\":[\"Islamic Art\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"islamic-art\"],\"dynasty\":null,\"description\":\"The monastic community of Bawit, founded in the 300s by Apa (Father) Apollo at a small village, grew to consist of numerous complexes with residences, chapels, and service structures and two large churches profusely decorated with sculptures and paintings, often reused from other sites, all affiliated with the Coptic church. The more important buildings were decorated with courses of sculpted stone freely combining floral and geometric motifs inspired by classical art – rosettes, acanthus leaves, meander patterns – with more recent Christian themes. These sculptural elements, demonstrating the Byzantine love of complex patterning with lush textures, were originally painted in rich, vibrant colors like those surviving in textiles.\",\"extendedStatuses\":[\"accessioned\"],\"gallery\":\"302\",\"geography\":[\"Egypt\",\"Bawit\"],\"geolocation\":[\"Africa\",\"Egypt\",\"Mersa Matrûh\",\"Bawiti\"],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":true,\"isOpenAccess\":true,\"materials\":[\"Limestone\",\"Sculpture\",\"Reliefs\",\"Friezes\",\"Fragments\"],\"medium\":\"Limestone; carved in relief\",\"objectName\":\"Frieze fragment\",\"period\":null,\"provenance\":null,\"reign\":null,\"sortArtist\":null,\"sortTitle\":\"fragment from a molding with acanthus clusters under arcades\",\"suggestionArtists\":[],\"suggestionGeolocations\":[{\"value\":\"Africa\"},{\"value\":\"Egypt\"},{\"value\":\"Mersa Matrûh\"},{\"value\":\"Bawiti\"}],\"suggestionMaterials\":[{\"value\":\"Limestone\"},{\"value\":\"Sculpture\"},{\"value\":\"Reliefs\"},{\"value\":\"Friezes\"},{\"value\":\"Fragments\"}],\"tags\":[],\"thumbnailAltText\":\"Fragment from a Molding with Acanthus Clusters under Arcades, Limestone; carved in relief\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/is/web-additional/09.217.3.JPG\",\"tmsDepartmentId\":318,\"title\":[\"Fragment from a Molding with Acanthus Clusters under Arcades\"],\"whenFrom\":400,\"whenTo\":599,\"yearsMinMax\":[400,599],\"yearRange\":{\"gte\":400,\"lte\":599}}}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"41:[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":10,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"09.217.12\",\"artistPrimary\":null,\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[],\"artistsFlattened\":[],\"classifications\":[\"Sculpture\"],\"culture\":null,\"crdCollectionIds\":[14],\"crdId\":445966,\"creditLine\":\"Rogers Fund, 1909\",\"dated\":\"4th–5th century\",\"departmentName\":[\"Islamic Art\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"islamic-art\"],\"dynasty\":null,\"description\":null,\"extendedStatuses\":[\"accessioned\"],\"gallery\":\"302\",\"geography\":[\"Egypt or Syria\"],\"geolocation\":[\"Asia\",\"Syria\"],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":true,\"isOpenAccess\":true,\"materials\":[\"Limestone\",\"Sculpture\",\"Reliefs\"],\"medium\":\"Limestone; carved\",\"objectName\":\"Relief\",\"period\":null,\"provenance\":null,\"reign\":null,\"sortArtist\":null,\"sortTitle\":\"fragment from a relief with the bust of a woman\",\"suggestionArtists\":[],\"suggestionGeolocations\":[{\"value\":\"Asia\"},{\"value\":\"Syria\"}],\"suggestionMaterials\":[{\"value\":\"Limestone\"},{\"value\":\"Sculpture\"},{\"value\":\"Reliefs\"}],\"tags\":[\"Women\"],\"thumbnailAltText\":\"Fragment from a Relief with the Bust of a Woman, Limestone; carved\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/is/web-additional/wb-09.217.12.JPG\",\"tmsDepartmentId\":318,\"title\":[\"Fragment from a Relief with the Bust of a Woman\"],\"whenFrom\":300,\"whenTo\":499,\"yearsMinMax\":[300,499],\"yearRange\":{\"gte\":300,\"lte\":499}}}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"42:[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":11,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"10.175.37\",\"artistPrimary\":null,\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[],\"artistsFlattened\":[],\"classifications\":[\"Sculpture\"],\"culture\":null,\"crdCollectionIds\":[14],\"crdId\":446114,\"creditLine\":\"Rogers Fund, 1910\",\"dated\":\"6th–7th century\",\"departmentName\":[\"Islamic Art\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"islamic-art\"],\"dynasty\":null,\"description\":\"In the 500s Apa (Father) Jeremias founded a Coptic monastery at Saqqara, the ancient Egyptian necropolis (cemetery) of the city of Memphis, near the oldest pyramids. He and the earliest monks lived in tombs at the site. As the monastery grew, several grand churches with lavish decoration were built, as well as many chapels, public buildings, and complexes of cells (rooms) for each monk. The monastery continued to grow after the Arab conquest with materials from early Byzantine tomb structures being reused for additional monastic structures. Inscriptions record prayers to numerous holy figures. Sculptural elements from Saqqara are often carved with deeply undercut patterns to intensify the play of light and shadow and to mask the solidity of the architecture. This sculptural style was popular throughout the Byzantine world in the 500s.\",\"extendedStatuses\":[\"accessioned\"],\"gallery\":\"302\",\"geography\":[\"Egypt\",\"Saqqara\"],\"geolocation\":[\"Africa\",\"Egypt\",\"Upper Egypt\",\"Saqqara\"],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":true,\"isOpenAccess\":true,\"materials\":[\"Limestone\",\"Sculpture\",\"Reliefs\",\"Fragments\"],\"medium\":\"Limestone; carved in relief\",\"objectName\":\"Fragment\",\"period\":null,\"provenance\":null,\"reign\":null,\"sortArtist\":null,\"sortTitle\":\"fragment from a molding with intertwined vines and acanthus leaves\",\"suggestionArtists\":[],\"suggestionGeolocations\":[{\"value\":\"Africa\"},{\"value\":\"Egypt\"},{\"value\":\"Upper Egypt\"},{\"value\":\"Saqqara\"}],\"suggestionMaterials\":[{\"value\":\"Limestone\"},{\"value\":\"Sculpture\"},{\"value\":\"Reliefs\"},{\"value\":\"Fragments\"}],\"tags\":[\"Leaves\",\"Vines\"],\"thumbnailAltText\":\"Fragment from a Molding with Intertwined Vines and Acanthus Leaves, Limestone; carved in relief\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/is/web-additional/sf10-175-37s1.jpg\",\"tmsDepartmentId\":318,\"title\":[\"Fragment from a Molding with Intertwined Vines and Acanthus Leaves\"],\"whenFrom\":500,\"whenTo\":699,\"yearsMinMax\":[500,699],\"yearRange\":{\"gte\":500,\"lte\":699}}}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"43:[\"$\",\"$L37\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":0,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"2006.355a–d\",\"artistPrimary\":\"Simon Costin\",\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[{\"constituentID\":27598,\"displayName\":\"Simon Costin\",\"nationality\":\"\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"role\":\"Designer\",\"roleID\":86}],\"artistsFlattened\":[\"Simon Costin\",\"British\"],\"classifications\":[],\"culture\":\"British\",\"crdCollectionIds\":[8],\"crdId\":130729,\"creditLine\":\"Alfred Z. Solomon-Janet A. Sloane Endowment Fund, 2006\",\"dated\":\"1987\",\"departmentName\":[\"The Costume Institute\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"the-costume-institute\"],\"dynasty\":null,\"description\":null,\"extendedStatuses\":[\"accessioned\"],\"gallery\":null,\"geography\":[],\"geolocation\":[\"Europe\",\"United Kingdom\"],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":false,\"isOpenAccess\":false,\"materials\":[\"Bone\",\"Metal\",\"Silver\",\"Crystal\",\"Quartz crystal\",\"Hematite\",\"Silk\",\"Pins\"],\"medium\":\"a) bone, silver, rock crystal and hematite; b) paper, silk; c) paper\",\"objectName\":\"Pin\",\"period\":null,\"provenance\":null,\"reign\":null,\"sortArtist\":\"costin simon\",\"sortTitle\":\"rabbit skull\",\"suggestionArtists\":[{\"value\":\"Simon Costin\"},{\"value\":\"British\"}],\"suggestionGeolocations\":[{\"value\":\"Europe\"},{\"value\":\"United Kingdom\"}],\"suggestionMaterials\":[{\"value\":\"Bone\"},{\"value\":\"Metal\"},{\"value\":\"Silver\"},{\"value\":\"Crystal\"},{\"value\":\"Quartz crystal\"},{\"value\":\"Hematite\"},{\"value\":\"Silk\"},{\"value\":\"Pins\"}],\"tags\":[],\"thumbnailAltText\":\"\\\"Rabbit Skull\\\", Simon Costin, a) bone, silver, rock crystal and hematite; b) paper, silk; c) paper, British\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/ci/web-additional/2006.355a%E2%80%93c_F.JPG\",\"tmsDepartmentId\":162,\"title\":[\"\\\"Rabbit Skull\\\"\"],\"whenFrom\":1987,\"whenTo\":1987,\"yearsMinMax\":[1987,1987],\"yearRange\":{\"gte\":1987,\"lte\":1987}}}],[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":1,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"25.3.169b\",\"artistPrimary\":null,\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[],\"artistsFlattened\":[],\"classifications\":[],\"culture\":null,\"crdCollectionIds\":[10],\"crdId\":694158,\"creditLine\":\"Rogers Fund, 1925\",\"dated\":\"ca. 1070–945 B.C.\",\"departmentName\":[\"Egyptian Art\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"egyptian-art\"],\"dynasty\":\"Dynasty 21\",\"description\":\"Nine amulets of various materials were found threaded together in a tight group around the throat of the Lady Djedmutesankh; two of these, of green glass, disintegrated after they were discovered. This is a wedjat, the mythological eye of Horus that was wounded and then magically made whole. It was thus considered a powerful symbol of healing and regeneration.For the amulet group, see 25.3.169-related.\",\"extendedStatuses\":[\"accessioned\"],\"gallery\":\"126\",\"geography\":[\"Egypt\",\"Upper Egypt, Thebes\",\"Deir el-Bahri\",\"Tomb MMA 60, Chamber\",\"MMA excavations, 1923–24\",\"Burial of Djedmutesankh (Ch5)\"],\"geolocation\":[\"Africa\",\"Egypt\",\"Upper Egypt\",\"Thebes\"],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":true,\"isOpenAccess\":true,\"materials\":[\"Hematite\",\"Amulets\"],\"medium\":\"Hematite\",\"objectName\":\"wedjat amulet\",\"period\":\"Third Intermediate Period\",\"provenance\":\"Museum excavations 1923–24. Acquired by the Museum in the division of finds, 1925.\",\"reign\":null,\"sortArtist\":null,\"sortTitle\":\"wedjat eye from string of amulets\",\"suggestionArtists\":[],\"suggestionGeolocations\":[{\"value\":\"Africa\"},{\"value\":\"Egypt\"},{\"value\":\"Upper Egypt\"},{\"value\":\"Thebes\"}],\"suggestionMaterials\":[{\"value\":\"Hematite\"},{\"value\":\"Amulets\"}],\"tags\":[],\"thumbnailAltText\":\"Wedjat eye from string of amulets, Hematite\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/eg/web-additional/25.3.169b_EGDP017215.jpg\",\"tmsDepartmentId\":229,\"title\":[\"Wedjat eye from string of amulets\"],\"whenFrom\":-1070,\"whenTo\":-945,\"yearsMinMax\":[-1070,-945],\"yearRange\":{\"gte\":-1070,\"lte\":-945}}}],[\"$\",\"$L38\",\"collectionObject-undefined\",{\"index\":2,\"enforceMaxWidth\":true,\"object\":{\"accessionNumber\":\"25.3.169c\",\"artistPrimary\":null,\"artistSecondary\":[],\"artists\":[],\"artistsFlattened\":[],\"classifications\":[],\"culture\":null,\"crdCollectionIds\":[10],\"crdId\":694159,\"creditLine\":\"Rogers Fund, 1925\",\"dated\":\"ca. 1070–945 B.C.\",\"departmentName\":[\"Egyptian Art\"],\"departmentSlug\":[\"egyptian-art\"],\"dynasty\":\"Dynasty 21\",\"description\":\"Nine amulets of various materials were found threaded together in a tight group around the throat of the Lady Djedmutesankh; two of these, of green glass, disintegrated after they were discovered. This is a wedjat, the mythological eye of Horus that was wounded and then magically made whole. It was thus considered a powerful symbol of healing and regeneration.For the amulet group, see 25.3.169-related.\",\"extendedStatuses\":[\"accessioned\"],\"gallery\":\"126\",\"geography\":[\"Egypt\",\"Upper Egypt, Thebes\",\"Deir el-Bahri\",\"Tomb MMA 60, Chamber\",\"MMA excavations, 1923–24\",\"Burial of Djedmutesankh (Ch5)\"],\"geolocation\":[\"Africa\",\"Egypt\",\"Upper Egypt\",\"Thebes\"],\"has3d\":false,\"hasImage\":true,\"hasNaziEraProvenance\":false,\"isHighlight\":false,\"isOnView\":true,\"isOpenAccess\":true,\"materials\":[\"Hematite\